        "point": "p = 0",
        "statement": "When p = 0, the predicate VTask.IsPGroup 0 G holds for every group G, because for any element g, taking k = 1 gives g ^ (0 ^ 1) = g ^ 0 = 1.",
        "note": "Edge case arising from 0 ^ k = 0 for k ≥ 1 and the group axiom g ^ 0 = 1."
      },
      {
        "point": "p = 1",
        "statement": "When p = 1, VTask.IsPGroup 1 G holds if and only if G is the trivial group, because 1 ^ k = 1 for all k, so the condition g ^ 1 = 1 must hold for every g.",
        "note": "Every element must be the identity."
      },
      {
        "point": "No primality assumption",
        "statement": "The definition does not require p to be prime; it is stated for any natural number p, though most structural theorems additionally assume Fact p.Prime.",
        "note": "Primality is a side condition on theorems, not on the definition itself."
      }
    ]
